 that security is a top issue for the  ukrainian government a ukrainian foreign  ministry spokesperson  said that  the ukrainian delegation would only  accept a solution that guarantees the  safety of its southern regions and the  secure export of its agricultural  products so russia would have to make a  promise a credible one not to attack  ukrainian ports and cargo ships the deal  also provides for the creation of a u.n  run  control center here in istanbul at the  strategic location  near the black sea  where brain exports would be overseen  the two sides have reportedly also  agreed on  inspecting  ships that are going to ukraine coming  from ukraine to make sure there are no  weapons or other military equipment on  board that's been a russian demand what  is less clear is right now  how and if ukraine would have to demine  its ports and what compromise has been  found on moscow's demand to ease  restrictions on russian exports of food  and fertilizers  now both sides have been at least  publicly very quiet about this deal are  there any doubt it will come to pass  well uh this deal if signed would be one  of the most significant compromises  reached between moscow and kiev in this  conflict so far there is just so much at  stake the united nations have repeatedly  warned of a global hunger crisis  affecting particularly countries in the  middle east and africa who depend on  ukrainian uh grain the turkish president  interestingly said any uh deal as soon  as it's reached a plan could be put into  action other have others have cautioned  that it might actually take a couple of  weeks to get ukrainian grain exports  going but then there is of course a  difference between what's written down  and signed on a piece of paper and the  situation the realities on the ground we  know the ukrainian government has  accused russia of intensifying strikes  on ukrainian cities in recent weeks so  what will be key at the end is of course  the implementation of this deal  our